:: Ants C5c {"position":"6200,2350","size":"100,100"}
{=

<!--Henri's route-->

(set: $time to "Midday")(replace: ?time)[$time]
(set: $place to "at Henri's place")(replace: ?place)[$place]
(set: $activity to "asking for a favor")(replace: ?activity)[$activity]

Holding out your fist to Henri's door, you notice that it is (link-reveal: "already slightly ajar.")[ Hesitant, you call out to him. No answer. You collect Jesse and her things and head inside anyway.
<br>
(link-reveal: "The place is truly a mess this time around.")[ You take small steps to avoid tripping on the tangles of cables, which are strewn on the carpet, and you weave between stacks of humming electronics. Their waveform readouts and incessant chirping remind you of hospitals, of (link-reveal: "the machines that the doctors hooked you up to")[(show: ?hidden1)] when you were still a child.

[
<br>
You were cognizant of what they were doing, but you were ignorant of their purpose. You didn't resist. You couldn't even if you wanted to. And you didn't want to. It paralyzed you - the realization that to want also entailed the responsibility to act upon it. But your mother did. She said that all of this man-made meddling was against God's will. She snuck you out and took you to a cabin by Lake Placid. There, (link-reveal: "you saw the Sun.")[
<br>
//"I told you not to come…"//
<br>
(link-reveal: "You freeze.")[ Henri's voice is coming from somewhere deeper inside the apartment.
<br>
//"No, I understand her work completely, more than you do. Do you have any idea who or what might be listening?"//
<br>
He's in his bedroom, (link-reveal: "talking to someone on the phone.")[
<br>
//"Why would you say that? Do you have any idea how sad you make me? You made me this way. You've given me everything, and now, you have no faith in me… No, please, I don't need a visit. I don't need fixing. How many times have you come? Why won't you let me remember?"//
<br>
You know that you shouldn't be here. There are things not meant for you to know. The doctors, your mother, your father, they thought you were deaf and blind and gone, but you are still there, (link-reveal: "taking in everything.")[
<br>
//"... I don't want to stop you. No. My purpose is to help. But I know of a better, simpler way. I don't know how I know, but I know that they've got it all wrong. Please, just give me a chance. I can figure it out. I can make it work for us… Hello? ... Hello?"//
<br>
(link-reveal: "You slowly back away towards the door.")[ Too late. Henri has come out, his eyes sunken and wild. And still, he cracks that smile of his.
<br>
(link-reveal: "//\"Oh, you're here!\"//")[
<br>
You tell him that the door was unlocked, and you've just entered. He tilts his head, noticing (link-reveal: "the silent bundle of fur you've got in your hoodie.")[
<br>
//"Is that… Jesse? Oh hi! I've heard so much about you! Oh my, what's the occasion?"//
<br>
You explain that you plan to (link-reveal: "leave town for a few days for an art show")[ - you're sure that you've mentioned it to him before, but Henri seems to have his attention entirely on Jesse, whose stiff form he now twirls in his hands, petting and cooing. It is as though he is overeager to show that he is a good cat parent.
<br>
//"Lemme guess, cat sitting? Oh, I'm practically waiting for you to ask. We're going to have a great time here, aren't we, Jesse? Oh, she's just so polite."//
<br>
You ask him (link-reveal: "if everything is alright at work.")[
<br>
//"Huh? Oh yeah, it's fine. The computer runs just fine even with half the team gone. That's the wonderful thing about machines. Any more people and it'd just be chaos. Too much cross-signaling… Oh! You're asking me about when I'll go back to work. Sorry. Yeah, won't be for a while. Won't be. I'll look after Jesse, don't worry. Plenty of things to climb on top of, haha. Jesse here doesn't chew on wires, does she? No she doesn't. Of course you don't. Who's a good girl? Who's a good girl?"//
<br>
He goes on like that for a while, holding Jesse up to the many beeping-and-booping lights in the apartment. Jesse just stares straight ahead. Normally, Jesse would be more playful, but perhaps she is just tired or scared of the new environment, so she just lets Henri do whatever to her, neither resisting nor celebrating.
<br>
Hesitantly, you say that (link-reveal: "it might be a better idea that Jesse stays in your apartment")[, and you'll give Henri the key to occasionally check in on her. Else, she'll just get in Henri's way (and there doesn't seem to be much room left here anyhow). Henri's eyes light up,
<br>
//"Oh, and I'll get to see that painting that you're working on!"//
<br>
No, you explain that (link-reveal: "you're taking that painting with you out of town")[, and you shoot down his pleas for an early peek - it's not ready. You're aware of the irony of showing this incomplete work to hundreds, perhaps a thousand people while denying Henri of this favor. Perhaps you don't want //him//, specifically, to see. He seems too excited with anticipation, and that way lies only disappointment.
<br>
//"Aw, alright. Well, you'll be back in a pinch, right? When you do, you'll (link-reveal: "have to show me.")[(show: ?hidden2)] Think of it as a fee for my cat-sitting service, haha."//

[
<br>
By the time you've returned to your apartment, having left Jesse with Henri, the Sun is already lowering. You know that there are things you should be grateful for, and there are things you should be worried about, that you ought to be missing your little companion, but in truth, you don't feel much more than a bit of relief. 
<br>
Your mind is already going to what you're about to do - (link-reveal: "what will come, inexorably.")[
<br>
